[135.744013, "i", "cat >> README.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Resume and durability\n\nThe checkpoint is the commit record. Each version-1 checkpoint includes the last\ncommitted document id, the committed row count, and byte lengths of all committed\nshards. The worker flushes buffered rows, fsyncs every affected shard (including\nshards closed during rotation), and syncs the index directory before publishing\nprogress. Checkpoint publication uses a temporary file, fsync, atomic rename,\nand a directory fsync; it never overwrites a live checkpoint in place.\n\nAt startup the worker removes shards and truncates partial appends beyond that\nfrontier, then resumes **strictly after** the last committed id. This also repairs\nan interrupted write or a crash between writing data and publishing progress.\nMissing/short committed shards are reported as errors rather than skipped.\nSIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP request a stop after the current document and commit the\npartial buffer; SIGKILL safely falls back to the previous committed frontier.\nNormal processing retains "]
[135.744484, "i", "block-sized writes. No background work escapes the\nsupervisor and its budgets, grace period, and cooldown are unchanged.\n\nCheckpoints from the old implementation cannot prove which rows reached disk;\nthey are rebuilt once using the new protocol. `./jobs clean` explicitly discards\nthe index and progress. As before, a completed checkpoint represents a rebuild\nof a fixed source corpus; clean before starting a new rebuild for source changes.\nEOF\n"]
[135.744567, "o", "cat >> README.md <<'EOF'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## Resume and durability\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> The checkpoint is the commit record. Each version-1 checkpoint includes the last"]
[135.746148, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> committed document id, the committed row count, and byte lengths of all committed\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> shards. The worker flushes buffered rows, fsyncs every affected shard (including\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> shards closed during rotation), and syncs the index directory before publishing\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> progress. Checkpoint publication uses a temporary file, fsync, atomic rename,\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> and a directory fsync; it never overwrites a live checkpoint in place.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> At startup the worker removes shards and truncates partial appends beyond that\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> frontier, then resumes **strictly after** the last committed id. This also repairs\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> an interrupted write or a crash between writing data and publishing progress.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Missing/short committed shards are reported as errors rather than skipped.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP request a stop after the current document and commit the\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?200"]
[135.746464, "o", "4h> partial buffer; SIGKILL safely falls back to the previous committed frontier.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Normal processing retains block-sized writes. No background work escapes the\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> supervisor and its budgets, grace period, and cooldown are unchanged.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Checkpoints from the old implementation cannot prove which rows reached disk;\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> they are rebuilt once using the new protocol. `./jobs clean` explicitly discards\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> the index and progress. As before, a completed checkpoint represents a rebuild\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> of a fixed source corpus; clean before starting a new rebuild for source changes.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> EOF\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
